<p><a class="environment-identifier" href="/tag/00NJ">Definition <span data-tag="00NJ">2.5.0.5</span> <span class="named">(Chain Homotopy)</span>.</a> Let $\operatorname{\mathcal{A}}$ be an additive category and let $(C_{\ast }, \partial _ C)$ and $(D_{\ast }, \partial _ D)$ be chain complexes with values in $\operatorname{\mathcal{A}}$. Let $f = \{  f_ n \} _{n \in \operatorname{\mathbf{Z}}}$ and $f' = \{  f'_ n \} _{n \in \operatorname{\mathbf{Z}}}$ be chain maps from $C_{\ast }$ to $D_{\ast }$. A <em>chain homotopy from $f$ to $f'$</em> is a collection of maps $h = \{  h_ n: C_ n \rightarrow D_{n+1} \} $ which satisfy the identity </p>
<div class="equation">
  \[ f'_ n - f_{n} = \partial _{D} \circ h_{n} + h_{n-1} \circ \partial _ C \]
</div>
<p> for every integer $n$. </p>
<p>We say that $f$ and $f'$ are <em>chain homotopic</em> if there exists a chain homotopy from $f$ to $f'$. We will say that $f$ is a <em>chain homotopy equivalence</em> if there exists a chain map $g: D_{\ast } \rightarrow C_{\ast }$ such that $g \circ f$ and $f \circ g$ are chain homotopic to the identity morphisms $\operatorname{id}_{ C_{\ast } }$ and $\operatorname{id}_{ D_{\ast } }$, respectively. </p>
</article>
